const narratives=[
{id:'calculus-war',title:'Two inventors. One bitter argument.',kicker:'Who gets to own an idea?',category:'Rivalries',period:'1660s to 1710s',people:['newton','leibniz'],discoveries:['calculus','analytic-geometry','gravity'],sources:['calculusStory','calculusDispute'],dek:'Newton developed his methods earlier. Leibniz published first. That difference became a battle over scientific reputation.',chapters:[
['The same mountain, two routes','A curve presented two stubborn questions: how steep is it at a particular point, and how much area lies beneath it? During the seventeenth century, Newton and Leibniz developed powerful ways to answer both. Newton thought in terms of quantities flowing with time. Leibniz built a language of tiny differences. Their mathematics grew from a much older landscape of problems and partial solutions.'],
['Discovery meets publication','Newton worked on his methods in the 1660s, but much remained unpublished. Leibniz published differential calculus in 1684. The dates created a dangerous gap between having an idea and making it available. A dispute over priority hardened into accusations and drew other mathematicians into competing camps. Letters and institutional authority became part of the contest.'],
['What survived the quarrel','The lasting result was larger than either man’s claim to first place. Newton’s approach connected mathematics closely to motion; Leibniz’s notation became deeply embedded in how calculus is written. Reading the controversy today, two questions separate: who developed which ideas, and who gained the power to tell the story?'] ],note:'Both developed calculus independently. The dispute is not evidence that one simply copied the other.'},
{id:'wallace-letter',title:'The letter that interrupted Darwin',kicker:'An idea arrives from far away',category:'Rivalries',period:'1858',people:['darwin','wallace'],discoveries:['evolution','geology-principles'],sources:['wallaceStory','wallaceMeeting'],dek:'Darwin had spent years developing natural selection. Then Wallace sent him an essay with an alarmingly familiar explanation.',chapters:[
['A familiar argument','In 1858, Charles Darwin received an essay from Alfred Russel Wallace, who was working in the Malay Archipelago. Wallace had reached a theory of natural selection independently. For Darwin, the scientific importance was inseparable from a personal crisis: an explanation he had developed over many years had arrived in somebody else’s words.'],
['A solution arranged by others','Charles Lyell and Joseph Hooker arranged a joint presentation. Wallace’s essay would appear alongside material establishing Darwin’s earlier thinking. On 1 July 1858, the papers were read at the Linnean Society. Neither author stood before the audience to deliver this moment in the history of biology.'],
['Two names, uneven memory','Darwin’s much fuller book followed in 1859 and became the work most closely associated with the theory. Wallace’s place can disappear when the story is shortened to a single great thinker. The correspondence makes that shortening difficult: ideas, distance, friendship and priority all mattered. This was competition handled through a negotiated presentation, not a simple tale of enemies.'] ],note:'The surviving correspondence records Wallace’s thanks to Hooker and Lyell. It does not justify inventing either a secret feud or perfect equality of influence.'},
{id:'photo51',title:'The photograph behind the famous model',kicker:'Evidence travels; credit does not always follow',category:'Credit & recognition',period:'1952 to 1953',people:['franklin','gosling','wilkins','watson','crick'],discoveries:['photo','helix','xray-crystals'],sources:['photo','dna'],dek:'A pattern of dark marks became a clue to DNA. Its journey between laboratories complicates the familiar discovery story.',chapters:[
['Making the image','At King’s College London, Rosalind Franklin and her doctoral student Raymond Gosling worked to obtain and interpret diffraction patterns from DNA. Photograph 51, made in 1952, recorded the B form of the molecule. It was the product of careful preparation and measurement, not an ordinary photograph revealing a tiny spiral.'],
['From pattern to structure','Watson saw the image through Wilkins early in 1953. The photograph and other experimental information helped constrain the model Watson and Crick were building in Cambridge. Their success depended on evidence developed by researchers beyond the pair whose names became most familiar.'],
['Three papers, a longer argument','In April 1953, Nature published the model alongside papers from the King’s researchers, including Franklin and Gosling. Later retellings often turn one striking image into the entire solution. That misses both the wider measurements and the difficult questions about how information was shared and whose contributions were acknowledged. A discovery can be intellectually connected without every participant being an equal or willing collaborator.'] ],note:'Photograph 51 was important, but it did not singlehandedly reveal every feature of the double helix. The atlas distinguishes evidence sharing from agreed collaboration.'},
{id:'winter-fission',title:'A nucleus breaks. A partnership is forced apart.',kicker:'Science across a border',category:'Collaboration',period:'1938 to 1939',people:['meitner','frisch','hahn','strassmann'],discoveries:['fission','neutron-irradiation','chain-reaction'],sources:['meitnerStory','meitnerWalk','fissionpaper'],dek:'Meitner had fled Nazi Germany. The puzzling results from her former laboratory still reached her by letter.',chapters:[
['An impossible chemical result','Late in 1938, Otto Hahn and Fritz Strassmann found something deeply puzzling after bombarding uranium: chemical evidence of barium, a much lighter element. Hahn wrote to Lise Meitner. Their long research partnership had been physically broken when persecution forced her to escape Germany for Sweden.'],
['An explanation in exile','Meitner discussed the result with her nephew Otto Frisch during his holiday visit. They worked out how a heavy nucleus could split and release a large amount of energy. Frisch later recalled discussing the problem during a walk in the snow. The remembered scene is vivid, but the essential achievement was the physical interpretation of the chemical evidence.'],
['A divided discovery','Experimental chemistry and theoretical physics had met across an enforced separation. The resulting papers opened a new field with enormous consequences. Reducing the discovery to one name erases the different tasks: detecting unexpected products, recognizing what they meant, and testing the explanation. It also erases the political violence that determined where the scientists could work.'] ],note:'The snowy walk comes from later recollections. The story does not reconstruct private dialogue or present every scenic detail as a contemporary record.'},
{id:'faraday-door',title:'The bookbinder who entered the laboratory',kicker:'A mentor opens a door',category:'Mentorship',period:'1812 to 1831',people:['davy','faraday','maxwell'],discoveries:['electrolysis','induction','fields'],sources:['faradayStory','faradayTension'],dek:'Faraday’s route into science ran through books, public lectures and an opportunity from Humphry Davy.',chapters:[
['Learning from the books he handled','Michael Faraday did not begin with a university research position. As a young bookbinder, he encountered scientific ideas through reading. Lectures at the Royal Institution brought him close to Humphry Davy’s experimental world. Faraday’s notes helped demonstrate a seriousness that a formal academic record could not.'],
['Inside the institution','Davy hired him as a chemical assistant in 1813. The appointment mattered: access to apparatus, practical work and a scientific community changed what Faraday could attempt. Yet mentorship did not make their relationship permanently harmonious. The Royal Institution’s history records later tensions as Faraday developed work of his own.'],
['Beyond the mentor’s shadow','In 1831, Faraday’s induction experiments established that changing magnetic conditions could produce an electric current. Later, Maxwell would give electromagnetic ideas a mathematical unity. The connection is more interesting than a parade of isolated geniuses: a lecture helped open a laboratory door, laboratory work produced new ideas, and another mind found a language for them.'] ],note:'Davy’s appointment of Faraday is documented. Famous flattering sayings attributed to mentors are not needed to establish the relationship.'},
{id:'ramanujan-letter',title:'An unfamiliar name in Hardy’s post',kicker:'A collaboration begins on paper',category:'Collaboration',period:'1913 to 1918',people:['ramanujan','hardy'],discoveries:['ramanujan-partitions'],sources:['ramanujanStory','ramanujanLetter','ramanujan'],dek:'The results were arresting. The proofs were another matter. A letter from India began an unusual mathematical partnership.',chapters:[
['Results without the usual introduction','In 1913, Srinivasa Ramanujan wrote to G. H. Hardy in Cambridge with mathematical results. He was outside the usual channels through which an established British mathematician encountered new colleagues. Hardy’s reply showed strong interest, but repeatedly asked for proofs and more detail. The exchange began with both recognition and unresolved questions.'],
['Two ways of working meet','Ramanujan arrived in Cambridge in 1914. Their collaboration brought together extraordinary mathematical intuition and Hardy’s insistence on rigorous argument. Calling it simply a teacher discovering a pupil understates what Ramanujan already knew and what each man contributed.'],
['A problem about breaking numbers apart','Among their achievements was work on partitions: the different ways a whole number can be written as a sum of positive whole numbers. Their formula made a difficult counting problem accessible through an unexpectedly powerful approximation. The route from letter to result shows why collaboration can be productive precisely when the participants do not think alike.'] ],note:'The story follows the documented correspondence and collaboration. It avoids treating scenes from later films as historical evidence.'},
{id:'curie-mineral',title:'The mineral was more active than it should be',kicker:'A measurement becomes a search',category:'Discoveries',period:'1898 to 1902',people:['marie','pierre','becquerel'],discoveries:['radiation','elements'],sources:['curieStory','curie'],dek:'If uranium explained the radiation, why did the mineral give such a strong signal? Marie Curie followed the mismatch.',chapters:[
['The number that would not fit','Becquerel’s discovery of uranium radiation gave Marie Curie a subject to investigate. Measuring minerals, she found activity too strong to be explained by their uranium content alone. Rather than smoothing away the discrepancy, she treated it as a clue that another, much more active substance might be present.'],
['Following the activity','Pierre joined the investigation. Chemical separation and repeated measurements allowed them to follow the radiation through different fractions of material. In 1898, the work led to announcements of polonium and radium. The evidence for new elements arrived before the arduous job of obtaining sufficiently pure material was complete.'],
['Discovery was only the beginning','The familiar image of a sudden breakthrough hides years of processing and measurement in poor working conditions. The partnership connected different skills, and the radiation measurement served as a guide through a chemical maze. What moved the investigation forward was not merely persistence, but a testable suspicion about a result that failed to match expectations.'] ],note:'Announcing an element and isolating it are different milestones. This story does not compress both into a single evening of discovery.'},
{id:'scruff-stars',title:'The signal that looked like a nuisance',kicker:'A student refuses to discard the oddity',category:'Credit & recognition',period:'1967',people:['bell','hewish'],discoveries:['pulsars'],sources:['pulsarStory','pulsarCredit'],dek:'Among long records from a radio telescope, Jocelyn Bell noticed a small disturbance that kept returning.',chapters:[
['An unglamorous place to look','The discovery began in the work of examining records. As a graduate student at Cambridge, Jocelyn Bell helped build a radio telescope and analysed its output. A small irregularity in the traces attracted her attention. It would have been easy to classify a troublesome feature as interference and move on.'],
['The irregularity becomes a rhythm','Follow up observations revealed remarkably regular pulses. The problem shifted from whether the signal was a nuisance to what could produce such a rhythm in the sky. Further sources helped establish that the phenomenon was astronomical. The signals became known as pulsars, associated with rapidly rotating neutron stars.'],
['Who appears in the headline?','Bell’s observation was central to opening the field. In 1974, the Nobel Prize recognized Antony Hewish and Martin Ryle; Bell Burnell was not included. That distinction between doing the work, leading a programme and receiving its most visible honour remains part of the story. The discovery asks the reader to notice the person who first noticed the evidence.'] ],note:'A briefly considered artificial origin is not evidence of an alien detection. The important story is the testing of an unexplained signal.'},
{id:'impossible-crystal',title:'The pattern the textbook ruled out',kicker:'When evidence challenges a definition',category:'Rivalries',period:'1982 to 2011',people:['shechtman'],discoveries:['quasicrystal','xray-crystals'],sources:['quasicrystalStory','quasicrystals'],dek:'Dan Shechtman saw ordered diffraction with a symmetry that conventional crystallography did not allow.',chapters:[
['The microscope disagrees','In 1982, Dan Shechtman examined an alloy with an electron microscope and encountered a pattern that did not fit the accepted picture of crystals. The issue was not that the material looked messy. It showed order of a kind that the conventional requirement of periodic repetition seemed to exclude.'],
['A difficult result to defend','Acceptance did not follow immediately. Shechtman faced strong opposition and was asked to leave his research group. The tension was scientific and personal: was the observation misleading, or was the inherited definition too narrow? Standing by a result meant little unless others could examine the evidence and alternative explanations.'],
['The definition changes','Quasicrystals eventually forced a broader understanding of ordered matter. Shechtman received the Nobel Prize in Chemistry in 2011. The easy moral would be that a lone dissenter is always right. The better one is harder: an unexpected result deserves careful testing, and a well established theory must remain answerable to the world.'] ],note:'Opposition alone does not validate a claim. Reproducible evidence, not the romance of rejection, changed crystallography.'},
{id:'copy-machine',title:'A conversation at the copying machine',kicker:'Two different problems meet',category:'Collaboration',period:'1990s to 2020',people:['kariko','weissman'],discoveries:['mrna-modified','mrna-vaccine','mrna'],sources:['copyMachine','mrna'],dek:'Karikó wanted RNA to become useful medicine. Weissman studied immune responses. Their interests met in a very ordinary place.',chapters:[
['An unlikely landmark','A miniature copying machine in the Nobel Prize Museum commemorates the beginning of Katalin Karikó and Drew Weissman’s collaboration. They met while copying scientific papers. The setting was mundane; the combination of expertise was not. RNA’s therapeutic promise met the problem of how the immune system reacts to it.'],
['The molecule was not enough','Getting cells to use introduced RNA required more than a useful genetic message. Unwanted immune activation could obstruct the approach. In 2005, their work showed that particular nucleoside modifications could reduce that response. A chemical change helped alter the biological reception of the message.'],
['A foundation, not a finished vaccine','Years later, modified RNA became one foundation of widely used mRNA vaccines. Delivery systems, manufacturing, clinical trials and many other teams were also essential. The copying machine makes a memorable beginning, but the scientific plot lies in combining questions that neither researcher could settle alone.'] ],note:'The chance encounter is documented by the Nobel Prize Museum. No dialogue has been invented, and the 2005 discovery is not presented as a complete vaccine.'},
{id:'einstein-box',title:'Einstein sets a trap for quantum theory',kicker:'A rivalry made of thought experiments',category:'Rivalries',period:'1930',people:['einstein','bohr','heisenberg'],discoveries:['uncertainty','general-relativity','photon'],sources:['quantumDebate'],dek:'Could a box of light expose a flaw in quantum uncertainty? Bohr’s answer turned to Einstein’s own theory of gravity.',chapters:[
['The imagined apparatus','Einstein proposed a box containing light, with a clock controlling a shutter. Let a photon escape, then weigh the box to work out the energy it had lost. If the clock also gave the emission time precisely, the arrangement appeared to challenge the quantum restriction on knowing both energy and time.'],
['The counterargument','Bohr’s response considered what the weighing operation would do to the clock’s situation in a gravitational field. General relativity connects clock rates with gravitational conditions. Accounting for that effect restored the uncertainty that Einstein’s argument seemed to evade. The defender of quantum theory had used a principle from Einstein’s own work.'],
['An argument larger than a winner','The exchange did not end all disputes about quantum mechanics. It made the assumptions of a proposed measurement more visible. Their disagreement matters because thought experiments could force vague objections into specific physical arrangements that others could analyse. The scientific value lies in that precision, not merely in declaring who won the conversation.'] ],note:'This is a thought experiment, not a report of a box built and tested in a laboratory. The account is simplified for a general reader.'},
{id:'four-day-year',title:'The planet with a four day year',kicker:'The rival observers check the result',category:'Discoveries',period:'1995',people:['mayor','queloz'],discoveries:['exoplanet-sun'],sources:['planetStory','planetLecture','mayorBio'],dek:'A giant planet so close to its star seemed surprising. Then another team pointed its telescope at the same target.',chapters:[
['A star that would not stay still','Michel Mayor and his doctoral student Didier Queloz searched for the small shifts in starlight that can reveal a companion’s gravitational pull. Around 51 Pegasi, the signal repeated on a startlingly short schedule: about four days. The inferred companion was a giant planet orbiting much closer to its star than Jupiter does to the Sun.'],
['Surprise is not confirmation','When the result was announced in 1995, it challenged expectations shaped by the Solar System. Other observers, including Geoffrey Marcy and Paul Butler, checked the star. Their observations supported the signal. Competition had supplied an especially useful response: an independent attempt to see whether the surprising result held up.'],
['A larger variety of worlds','51 Pegasi b helped establish that planetary systems could be very different from our own. Mayor and Queloz had found a planet around a Sun like star; planets around a pulsar had already been discovered. The distinction keeps the achievement impressive without turning it into an inaccurate claim to every kind of first.'] ],note:'The planet was inferred from the star’s motion, not directly photographed in this discovery.'},
{id:'sarabhai-detour',title:'The war detour that brought Sarabhai to Raman',kicker:'A research path through Bangalore',category:'Mentorship',period:'1940 to 1942',people:['sarabhai','raman'],discoveries:['cosmic-india','raman-scattering'],sources:['indiaspace'],dek:'War interrupted Sarabhai’s time at Cambridge. Returning to India brought him into Raman’s laboratory and into cosmic ray research.',chapters:[
['An interrupted journey','When war broke out in Europe, Vikram Sarabhai’s studies at Cambridge were disrupted. He returned to India in 1940 and went to the Indian Institute of Science. He could continue postgraduate research under C. V. Raman’s supervision. A change forced by world events became a new research setting.'],
['A place mattered to the question','Cosmic rays became Sarabhai’s focus. South India’s position near the magnetic equator made it an interesting location for measurements. Wartime difficulties limited plans involving balloons and other apparatus, and Sarabhai worked with a Geiger counter. The research was shaped by both an ambitious question and the equipment available.'],
['From a student’s work to institutions','In 1942, Raman introduced Sarabhai’s first paper to the Indian Academy of Sciences. Sarabhai later became a central figure in establishing India’s space programme and research institutions. The link in this atlas is specific: Raman supervised his early research. It is not a claim that every later achievement flowed from one mentor alone.'] ],note:'This account follows IISc’s published historical narrative. Later institutional achievements are not assigned to the 1940 milestone.'}
];
// Rivalries are symmetric relationships, distinct from influence and mentorship.
peopleEdges.push(['newton','leibniz','Rivalry','A prolonged priority dispute concerned their independently developed calculus.','calculusDispute']);
peopleEdges.push(['einstein','bohr','Debate','They challenged one another’s interpretation of quantum mechanics through thought experiments.','quantumDebate']);
peopleEdges.push(['hewish','bell','Mentorship','Bell was a doctoral student in Hewish’s radio astronomy group when she identified the first pulsar signal.','pulsarStory']);
